Enable the MiniLoong Pocket1 external speaker amplifier.

The board gates its speaker through an amplifier enabled by gpio0 RK_PC0. That
pin appears three times in the DTS and is dead in all three: commented out in
the rk817 codec node, commented out inside a disabled block, and present on the
mangmi-powerctrl node which is status = "disabled". Nothing ever drives it, so
the pin floats low and the amplifier stays powered off.

The result is silent playback that looks entirely healthy from software -
'Playback Path' can be set to SPK, DAC volume reads 80%, and RetroArch holds
/dev/snd/pcmC0D0p open - with no sound at all. Driving the pin high by hand
from sysfs restores audio immediately, confirming the amplifier is the only
thing missing.

Hand it to the codec driver instead of a board hack: rk817_codec.c picks it up
as an optional "spk-ctl" gpiod and toggles it from rk817_codec_ctl_gpio() when
the playback path changes, so the speaker is also muted correctly once
headphone detection routes audio away from it.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-miniloong.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-miniloong.dts
@@ -1024,8 +1024,17 @@
 			hp-volume = <20>;
 			spk-volume = <3>;
 			out-l2spk-r2hp;
-			/* Miniloong external speaker amp enable */
-			//spk-ctl-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			/*
+			 * External speaker amp enable. Without this the codec
+			 * drives its output into an amplifier that is never
+			 * powered, so playback runs with correct mixer state
+			 * and a busy PCM but makes no sound at all. The codec
+			 * driver takes it as an optional "spk-ctl" gpiod and
+			 * toggles it from rk817_codec_ctl_gpio() on playback
+			 * path changes, so the speaker also mutes properly
+			 * when headphones are inserted.
+			 */
+			spk-ctl-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 			/* Miniloong headphone detect */
 			//hp-det-gpios = <&gpio4 RK_PC6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 			status = "okay";
